design:
## Agreed scope and ownership

Implement a condensed target data layout for x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu first. Future architecture families are x86_64, i686, aarch64, and ARM32; select by complete target triple/ABI, not architecture name alone. Inspect Clang's target facts, existing predefines, and small sizeof/_Alignof probes rather than copying Clang's full TargetInfo hierarchy. This task does not require a runtime Clang dependency or a general target-description generator.

Storage information is retained for C sema: constant evaluation of sizeof/_Alignof and subsequent record layout. It is available in the IR dump but Rust emission can ignore it where rustc already provides the matching target layout. The user already handles f80 through external-call shims; do not introduce a new f80 implementation or require Rust emission to consume storage metadata for that path.

Existing concrete IR types (f64/f80/f128 etc.) already define numeric semantics. Do not add ScalarRepresentation or duplicate floating-point semantics/encoding fields. Select the existing concrete FloatType for C long double and store size/alignment separately.

## Proposed interface

```rust
pub struct TargetInfo {
    pub triple: String,
    pub endian: Endian,
    pub scalars: ScalarLayouts,
    pub pointer: StorageLayout,
    pub long_double: FloatType,
}

pub struct StorageLayout {
    pub size_bytes: u64,
    pub alignment_bytes: u32,
}

pub enum Endian {
    Little,
    Big,
}
```

Reuse the existing ir::FloatType and existing scalar type identities. ScalarLayouts is a lookup keyed by existing concrete scalar types (bool and NumericType), exposing a fallible storage query. Its container is an implementation choice; use deterministic printing independent of map iteration. Missing/unsupported types must return an explicit thiserror error, not a guessed bits/8 layout or an assertion.

Keep C type interpretation separate from this storage lookup. The existing Clang predefines supply integer widths/signedness and the underlying types for size_t, ptrdiff_t, wchar_t, char16_t, and char32_t where applicable. Resolve those through the existing predefines/configuration path; do not introduce a redundant CTypeMappings table. C integer ranks and typedef identities still belong to sema/source type information even where concrete types coincide.

EnumPolicy is not a field of this target-data interface. Enum selection belongs in C sema using compiler flavor, options (such as short-enum selection), enumerator values, and supported integer types. The resolved enum receives its concrete underlying type. Record/bit-field algorithms belong in the layout implementation selected for the target; no generic RecordPolicy property bag is needed. Default calling ABI follows the target; signature overrides belong on functions/function pointers, not in a duplicate CallingAbi field here.

The initial ordinary pointer layout is sufficient for the supported default address space. Do not silently apply it to an unsupported nondefault address space.

## Storage contract

All sizes and alignments are in bytes. Numeric type widths retain their existing bit units. Size is C object size, including padding, and determines array element stride. Alignment is the ordinary type ABI alignment, not preferred optimization alignment or explicit object/member alignment.

Use Clang facts for supported configurations. In particular:
- x86_64 Linux f80 long double: size 16, alignment 16.
- i686 Linux f80 long double: size 12, alignment 4.
- f64: size 8 on both; alignment 8 on x86_64 Linux and 4 on i686 Linux.
These were checked using installed Clang 22.1.8. They demonstrate why concrete value format alone is insufficient for C constant evaluation, even though Rust emission can rely on its backend/shim.

Resolve layout-changing options before preprocessing and sema so target predefines, long-double type selection, and storage queries agree. Preserve existing -mlong-double-64/80/128 behavior. Avoid multiple mutable sources of truth between src/target_info.rs and src/target/. Migrate or re-export the existing type as needed; do not leave two independently configured target descriptions.

## Textual representation

The target block is a compact dump of effective C storage facts, not a prescription for Rust code generation. Print stable lowercase type names, decimal byte counts, fixed entry order, and a quoted triple. Signed/unsigned pairs may share a line when their layouts match. Print only supported scalar entries.

Proposed baseline syntax:

```text
module {
    target "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu" {
        endian = little;
        pointer [size=8, align=8];
        long_double = f80;

        storage bool [size=1, align=1];
        storage i8, u8 [size=1, align=1];
        storage i16, u16 [size=2, align=2];
        storage i32, u32 [size=4, align=4];
        storage i64, u64 [size=8, align=8];
        storage i128, u128 [size=16, align=16];

        storage f16 [size=2, align=2];
        storage f32 [size=4, align=4];
        storage f64 [size=8, align=8];
        storage f80 [size=16, align=16];
        storage f128 [size=16, align=16];
    }

    fn %0 @object_size() -> u64 [linkage=external] {
        return const<u64>(16);
    }

    fn %1 @object_alignment() -> u64 [linkage=external] {
        return const<u64>(16);
    }

    fn %2 @one() -> f80 [linkage=external] {
        return const<f80>(1);
    }
}
```

Source for the functions above:

```c
unsigned long object_size(void) {
    return sizeof(long double);
}

unsigned long object_alignment(void) {
    return _Alignof(long double);
}

long double one(void) {
    return 1.0L;
}
```

With --show-metadata, retain the folded query's origin using the existing NodeId-keyed metadata mechanism:

```text
return const<u64>(16) [size_of="long double"];
return const<u64>(16) [align_of="long double"];
```

These keys are optional source context; the numeric constant remains meaningful without them. Preserve original spelling/expansion spans on the folded value. Ordinary source metadata already printed by module lowering may also appear; the example shows the relevant query annotations.

## Implementation boundary and validation

1. Add triple selection and effective target storage queries; reject unsupported triples rather than falling back to host layout. Keep the initial supported target explicit.
2. Wire preprocessing, TranslationUnit.target, sema Context, and Module.target to the same resolved configuration. Reuse existing numeric format types.
3. Add scalar type-name sizeof/_Alignof lowering directly in src/sema, producing a constant with the configured size_t concrete type. Keep AST sizeof/_Alignof nodes unevaluated; do not move this into parser/const_expr.
4. Thread query provenance into the existing module metadata table. The numeric Context currently returns Value without a metadata sink, so explicitly arrange metadata collection rather than losing it.
5. Use a no-parameter function fixture like the example: current module lowering supports it, so this acceptance slice need not wait for general declaration lowering.
6. Keep general record/union/enum layout in lh7.1.5 and broader sizeof-expression/VLA/offsetof folding in lh7.1.8. Unsupported incomplete/function/void or runtime-size queries must not be fabricated as constants.
7. Update src/ir/module_print.rs and the IR concept docs with the implemented interface and syntax. Preserve existing numeric-format and long-double-option fixtures.
8. Add FileCheck fixtures and generate expectations with python3 tools/update_filecheck.py --in-place <fixture>; no hand-written CHECK lines or unit tests. Check baseline scalar sizes/alignments and long-double option consistency against Clang.
9. Run repository Rust gates: cargo clippy --allow-dirty --fix, cargo fmt, cargo nextest run. Follow repository logging/commit protocol for the eventual implementation.

Ticket remains open until triple lookup and a real sema sizeof/_Alignof FileCheck fixture work; existing numeric widths/long-double literal support alone do not complete it.

description:
clang's builtin stdatomic.h (and other clang resource-dir headers) wrap C++ interop blocks in:
  #ifdef __cplusplus
  extern "C" {
  #endif
  ...
  #ifdef __cplusplus
  }
  #endif

Since #ifdef/#ifndef conditions are kept symbolic (Condition::Defined) for later per-config resolution (the polyvariant design), the parser must be able to grammatically parse BOTH branches' text as valid C during the initial polyvariant-AST build, even though __cplusplus is never actually defined for a C compile. That means 'extern "C" { ... }' has to parse as a (no-op, in C) linkage-specification block, but currently the declaration parser hits it as if starting a normal declaration and fails with 'expected declaration type, found StringLit("C")' at the 'extern "C" {' line.

Repro: ./target/debug/slate-parser parse tests/upstream/fixtures/atomic_widths.c -isystem ~/Projects/slate/libc-shim/include -isystem /usr/lib/clang/22/include (also reproduces via tests/upstream/fixtures/c11.c). Minimal repro without headers:
  extern "C" {
  int x;
  }

Found while sweeping tests/upstream/fixtures under the real -isystem header chain for slate-parser-wf8.1.13/.14.

An object's size is not available on the constant-expression path, so an array bound written with sizeof of a variable fails:

  struct s3 { char a[3]; };
  struct s3 g;
  int arr[sizeof(g)];
  target/release/slate-parser ir t.c
  Observed: unsupported in numeric IR lowering: unknown or unsupported sizeof operand type

TypeResolver::assertion_operand_type looks the name up in the resolver's own scope, which only holds objects declared by the assertion pre-pass (sema/assertion.rs), not file-scope declarations seen by the module pass. _Static_assert(sizeof(g) == 3) works; an array bound does not. Predates slate-parser-lh7.2.20, found while threading _Atomic layout through that path.

Acceptance: a fixture declares an object and uses sizeof/_Alignof of it as an array bound and in a static assertion; both resolve.

enum E { A };
struct S { enum E x; };
struct S { unsigned x; };

clang 22.1.8 -std=c23 accepts; gcc 16.2.1 -std=c23 rejects ('redefinition of struct or union'). MSVC cannot vote: it has no C23 mode here and rejects any tag redefinition with C2011 under /std:c17, so the usual three-compiler consensus rule does not resolve this.

clang has the better standards argument: C23 requires the members of a compatible redefinition to have compatible types, and 6.7.2.2 makes an enumerated type compatible with its underlying integer type. slate-parser currently rejects, matching gcc.

The rejection is incidental rather than decided. After slate-parser-9ve.6, member type identity is decided on C types in TypeResolver::same_field_types (which would ACCEPT this, since CTypes::compatible knows enum/underlying compatibility). What rejects it is the residual structural pre-filter in same_tag_content, which still compares ir::Type field types -- Type::Defined(enum) vs u32 -- and is documented as the one remaining ir::Type comparison that can influence a C-level decision.

To change behaviour: drop the  clause from same_tag_content and let same_field_types decide. Deliberately not done during the audit, because flipping behaviour on a 1-1 compiler split needs a decision, not a sweep.
